<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corfinium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corfinium was the chief town of the Paeligni in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a> mountains of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, near modern Corfinio. In 91 BCE the Italian allies who revolted against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> made it their capital and renamed it Italia. In 49 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> besieged and took it from Domitius Ahenobarbus early in the civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corfinium.jpg" alt="Corfinium" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marica Massaro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morroni_di_Corfinium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Corfinium stood in the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aterno-pescara">Aternus</a>, near the point where the river turns sharply from a south-easterly to a north-easterly course towards the Adriatic Sea. It lay 7 miles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulmona">Sulmo</a> (modern Sulmona) and 30 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-fucens">Alba Fucens</a> by the Via Valeria. The bridge over the Aternus, 5 km from the city, is mentioned by both Caesar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and was always a military point of the highest importance; the town of Popoli grew up beside it, which led some authors wrongly to place Corfinium there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Social War the confederates probably chose Corfinium for its military position, and seem to have meant it as the permanent capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and a rival to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, giving it a new and spacious forum, a senate house and other public buildings. Before the end of the second year of the war they had to abandon it and move their seat of government to Aesernia (modern Isernia).</p><p>In 49 BCE Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus held the fortress with 31 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, the only place that offered Caesar effective resistance in his advance through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Disaffection among his officers forced Domitius to surrender after a siege of only seven days, and Caesar released the leading Republicans captured with the garrison after they swore loyalty, oaths many soon broke.</p><p>Under the Empire Corfinium remained a flourishing municipium, never a colony, though it received settlers in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gracchi">Gracchi</a> and again under Augustus. Its people built two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a>, largely cut in solid rock, one through a tunnel nearly 5 km long. In the 4th century it seems to have been the capital of the province of Valeria and the residence of its governor, and it was still in existence as late as the tenth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town was later known as Pentima and then, in medieval times, as Valva, a name the neighbouring valley, La Pianata di Valva, still keeps. It took back its ancient name in 1928, and ruins of Corfinium have been found around the modern town of Corfinio.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Corfinium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rebels set up a federal state modelled on Rome, with <b>Corfinium</b> as its capital, renamed Italia, a senate of 500 members and two consuls, the Marsian Quintus Poppaedius Silo and the Samnite Gaius Papius Mutilus.</blockquote><a href="/state/italia-social-war-confederation">Italia (Social War confederation)</a></li><li><blockquote>Their main towns were <b>Corfinium</b>, Sulmo and Superaequum, small hill communities that formed a league.</blockquote><a href="/state/paeligni">Paeligni</a></li></ul></section>
